1
00:00:00,403 --> 00:00:03,025
You open your phone, tap a name, and press

2
00:00:03,045 --> 00:00:03,772
call.

3
00:00:03,792 --> 00:00:06,125
A few seconds later, a phone rings on the

4
00:00:06,176 --> 00:00:07,349
other side of the planet.

5
00:00:07,774 --> 00:00:10,022
And when your friend says hello, their voice

6
00:00:10,042 --> 00:00:12,104
reaches you in about a quarter of a second.

7
00:00:12,529 --> 00:00:15,357
In that tiny moment, your voice turns into

8
00:00:15,377 --> 00:00:18,308
numbers, flies through the air as invisible

9
00:00:18,328 --> 00:00:21,002
waves, races through glass cables under

10
00:00:21,022 --> 00:00:23,431
the ocean, and turns back into sound.

11
00:00:23,857 --> 00:00:26,788
Let's follow one hello, step by step, from

12
00:00:26,808 --> 00:00:28,006
your mouth to their ear.

13
00:00:32,202 --> 00:00:33,246
It all starts with sound.

14
00:00:33,684 --> 00:00:36,339
When you speak, your voice pushes the air,

15
00:00:36,648 --> 00:00:38,465
making tiny waves of pressure.

16
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:41,654
Inside your phone's microphone, a thin layer

17
00:00:41,674 --> 00:00:44,283
shakes with those waves, and turns the shaking

18
00:00:44,303 --> 00:00:45,617
into an electrical signal.

19
00:00:46,055 --> 00:00:47,370
But phones don't send waves.

20
00:00:47,808 --> 00:00:48,620
They send numbers.

21
00:00:49,058 --> 00:00:51,293
So your phone measures that signal sixteen

22
00:00:51,313 --> 00:00:53,857
thousand times every second, and writes

23
00:00:53,877 --> 00:00:55,939
down each measurement as a number.

24
00:00:56,377 --> 00:00:57,370
This is called sampling.

25
00:00:57,808 --> 00:01:00,011
Sending all of those numbers would be wasteful,

26
00:01:00,398 --> 00:01:02,775
so a clever program called a codec squeezes

27
00:01:02,795 --> 00:01:03,595
them down.

28
00:01:03,723 --> 00:01:06,216
Instead of every number, it sends a short

29
00:01:06,236 --> 00:01:08,754
description of your voice, twenty milliseconds

30
00:01:08,774 --> 00:01:09,574
at a time.

31
00:01:09,754 --> 00:01:12,182
That's fifty little packets of voice, every

32
00:01:12,202 --> 00:01:13,002
single second.

33
00:01:13,375 --> 00:01:14,175
And they are tiny.

34
00:01:14,522 --> 00:01:16,589
A whole minute of talking is only about

35
00:01:16,609 --> 00:01:17,692
a hundred kilobytes.

36
00:01:18,117 --> 00:01:19,496
That's smaller than one photo.

37
00:01:23,702 --> 00:01:25,661
Now those packets need to leave your phone.

38
00:01:26,086 --> 00:01:28,386
Your phone's antenna turns them into radio

39
00:01:28,406 --> 00:01:29,206
waves.

40
00:01:29,334 --> 00:01:31,221
You can't see them, but they're the same

41
00:01:31,241 --> 00:01:33,785
kind of energy as light, so they travel

42
00:01:33,805 --> 00:01:35,667
at three hundred thousand kilometres per

43
00:01:35,687 --> 00:01:36,487
second.

44
00:01:36,524 --> 00:01:38,109
They head to the nearest cell tower.

45
00:01:38,535 --> 00:01:40,950
Your city is divided into small areas called

46
00:01:40,970 --> 00:01:43,328
cells, and each cell has a tower.

47
00:01:43,767 --> 00:01:45,055
That's why it's called a cell phone.

48
00:01:45,519 --> 00:01:47,587
Thousands of phones share each tower at

49
00:01:47,607 --> 00:01:48,407
the same time.

50
00:01:48,779 --> 00:01:51,169
To keep them apart, the tower gives every

51
00:01:51,189 --> 00:01:53,927
phone its own tiny slices of frequency and

52
00:01:53,947 --> 00:01:56,362
time, like giving each car its own lane

53
00:01:56,382 --> 00:01:57,182
on a highway.

54
00:01:57,504 --> 00:01:59,455
And if you're talking while moving, say

55
00:01:59,475 --> 00:02:02,239
in a car, your call is quietly passed from

56
00:02:02,259 --> 00:02:03,560
one tower to the next.

57
00:02:03,999 --> 00:02:06,169
This is called a handover, and it happens

58
00:02:06,189 --> 00:02:08,161
so smoothly that you never notice.

59
00:02:12,370 --> 00:02:13,170
But wait.

60
00:02:13,259 --> 00:02:15,708
Before any voice can travel, the network

61
00:02:15,849 --> 00:02:17,460
has to find the person you're calling.

62
00:02:17,898 --> 00:02:20,005
When you press call, your phone first sends

63
00:02:20,025 --> 00:02:22,414
a request, a small digital message that

64
00:02:22,434 --> 00:02:24,561
says: I want to talk to this number.

65
00:02:24,986 --> 00:02:27,048
It goes to your operator's core network,

66
00:02:27,306 --> 00:02:28,646
the brain of the whole system.

67
00:02:29,084 --> 00:02:30,894
The network already knows you're a real

68
00:02:30,914 --> 00:02:33,368
customer, because your SIM card proved who

69
00:02:33,388 --> 00:02:35,025
you are when your phone switched on.

70
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:36,996
Next, it reads the number.

71
00:02:37,434 --> 00:02:39,354
The country code tells it which country,

72
00:02:39,651 --> 00:02:41,442
and the rest tells it which operator.

73
00:02:41,867 --> 00:02:43,961
So your request is passed across to that

74
00:02:43,981 --> 00:02:44,781
operator.

75
00:02:45,025 --> 00:02:46,468
But how do they know where the phone is?

76
00:02:46,893 --> 00:02:48,896
Every phone that's switched on keeps telling

77
00:02:48,916 --> 00:02:50,772
the network which area it's in.

78
00:02:51,197 --> 00:02:52,782
So the operator knows where to look.

79
00:02:53,208 --> 00:02:55,301
It sends a message through every tower in

80
00:02:55,321 --> 00:02:57,769
that area: phone, you have a call.

81
00:02:58,208 --> 00:03:00,443
The phone hears it, wakes up, and starts

82
00:03:00,463 --> 00:03:01,203
ringing.

83
00:03:01,223 --> 00:03:03,427
On your side, you hear the ringing tone.

84
00:03:03,852 --> 00:03:06,152
And when your friend picks up, a path opens

85
00:03:06,172 --> 00:03:07,396
between the two phones.

86
00:03:07,821 --> 00:03:10,218
All of this usually takes just a few seconds.

87
00:03:14,433 --> 00:03:15,954
Now the voice packets can flow.

88
00:03:16,405 --> 00:03:18,447
From the tower, they don't stay in the air

89
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:19,267
for long.

90
00:03:19,472 --> 00:03:21,681
Most towers are connected by fibre optic

91
00:03:21,701 --> 00:03:22,501
cables.

92
00:03:22,655 --> 00:03:24,735
These are glass threads, thinner than a

93
00:03:24,755 --> 00:03:27,699
human hair, that carry data as flashes of

94
00:03:27,719 --> 00:03:28,519
light.

95
00:03:28,544 --> 00:03:31,018
The light bounces along inside the glass,

96
00:03:31,315 --> 00:03:34,317
even around bends, for thousands of kilometres.

97
00:03:34,742 --> 00:03:37,197
Along the way, machines called routers read

98
00:03:37,217 --> 00:03:39,593
the address on each packet, and send it

99
00:03:39,613 --> 00:03:41,211
onward, hop by hop.

100
00:03:41,650 --> 00:03:43,717
To cross an ocean, your call doesn't go

101
00:03:43,737 --> 00:03:44,537
to space.

102
00:03:44,871 --> 00:03:45,979
It dives under the sea.

103
00:03:46,405 --> 00:03:48,305
More than ninety five percent of the data

104
00:03:48,325 --> 00:03:50,728
between continents travels through cables

105
00:03:50,748 --> 00:03:52,461
lying on the ocean floor.

106
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:54,606
There are hundreds of them, more than a

107
00:03:54,626 --> 00:03:56,315
million kilometres in total.

108
00:03:56,753 --> 00:03:58,704
That's enough to wrap around the Earth thirty

109
00:03:58,724 --> 00:03:59,524
times.

110
00:03:59,742 --> 00:04:02,493
A call from India to America might travel

111
00:04:02,513 --> 00:04:05,508
through the Arabian Sea, past Egypt, across

112
00:04:05,528 --> 00:04:07,693
the Mediterranean, and under the Atlantic.

113
00:04:08,119 --> 00:04:10,831
Inside the glass, light moves at about two

114
00:04:10,851 --> 00:04:12,771
hundred thousand kilometres per second,

115
00:04:13,093 --> 00:04:15,367
so even this long trip takes only about

116
00:04:15,387 --> 00:04:16,392
a tenth of a second.

117
00:04:16,830 --> 00:04:19,233
Satellites can carry calls too, but they

118
00:04:19,253 --> 00:04:21,784
orbit thirty six thousand kilometres above

119
00:04:21,804 --> 00:04:22,570
us.

120
00:04:22,590 --> 00:04:24,709
Going up and coming back down adds about

121
00:04:24,729 --> 00:04:26,263
a quarter of a second each way.

122
00:04:26,701 --> 00:04:27,693
That's why cables win.

123
00:04:31,899 --> 00:04:34,044
On the other side, the packets reach your

124
00:04:34,064 --> 00:04:36,384
friend's operator, then the tower near them,

125
00:04:36,744 --> 00:04:38,407
and fly through the air to their phone.

126
00:04:38,871 --> 00:04:41,049
Here, everything happens in reverse.

127
00:04:41,487 --> 00:04:44,315
The codec unpacks each packet and rebuilds

128
00:04:44,335 --> 00:04:45,135
the numbers.

129
00:04:45,417 --> 00:04:47,871
A converter turns those numbers back into

130
00:04:47,891 --> 00:04:48,948
an electrical signal.

131
00:04:49,386 --> 00:04:51,918
That signal drives the speaker, which shakes

132
00:04:51,938 --> 00:04:54,386
the air, and your friend hears your voice.

133
00:04:54,824 --> 00:04:56,577
But networks aren't perfectly smooth.

134
00:04:57,015 --> 00:04:59,289
Packets can arrive a little early, a little

135
00:04:59,309 --> 00:05:00,610
late, or not at all.

136
00:05:01,036 --> 00:05:02,936
So the phone keeps a small waiting area

137
00:05:02,956 --> 00:05:04,013
called a jitter buffer.

138
00:05:04,438 --> 00:05:06,944
It holds packets for a split second, so

139
00:05:06,964 --> 00:05:08,510
it can play them in a steady rhythm.

140
00:05:08,935 --> 00:05:11,196
If a packet goes missing, the codec makes

141
00:05:11,216 --> 00:05:12,956
a smart guess to fill the gap.

142
00:05:13,394 --> 00:05:15,526
But when the network is bad and too many

143
00:05:15,546 --> 00:05:18,252
go missing, that's when you hear that robotic,

144
00:05:18,446 --> 00:05:19,399
broken voice.

145
00:05:23,612 --> 00:05:24,772
So let's put it all together.

146
00:05:25,197 --> 00:05:26,615
Your voice becomes numbers.

147
00:05:27,053 --> 00:05:29,765
The numbers become radio waves, flying to

148
00:05:29,785 --> 00:05:30,816
the nearest tower.

149
00:05:31,254 --> 00:05:33,115
The network finds your friend's phone and

150
00:05:33,135 --> 00:05:33,935
makes it ring.

151
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:36,414
Your packets race through fibre, and under

152
00:05:36,434 --> 00:05:37,234
the ocean.

153
00:05:37,401 --> 00:05:40,004
Then back into the air, and back into sound.

154
00:05:40,429 --> 00:05:42,948
And all of this happens fifty times every

155
00:05:42,968 --> 00:05:45,525
second, in both directions, for the whole

156
00:05:45,545 --> 00:05:46,324
call.

157
00:05:46,344 --> 00:05:48,231
So the next time you say hello to someone

158
00:05:48,251 --> 00:05:51,466
far away, remember: your voice just travelled

159
00:05:51,486 --> 00:05:53,811
across the world, and arrived in about a

160
00:05:53,831 --> 00:05:54,836
quarter of a second.
